// FrontierCS 2 task pages: how a model's final submission solves the task, in words, in place of its diff
// (owner, 2026-10-14). Keyed by the task's short name; m is the model, steps are [title, text]. Written from the
// patch in assets/patches/<slug>.json (bin/fcs2_patches_from_platform.py). A task with no entry shows no Solution section.
const SOLUTION = {
  "RAGEN": {m:'opus', steps:[
    ["Finding the turns", "The packed row is one chat: the system prompt, then the user and the model in turn. It counts the chat template’s turn-start tokens along the row, so every token knows which turn it belongs to. The loss mask covers everything after the system prompt, and the response mask covers only the model’s replies."],
    ["Placing the reward", "A game’s reward goes on its last token or, with rewards per turn, on the last token of each reply. Masks and rewards then shift by one token, so that each lines up with the token the model predicts."],
    ["Parsing replies", "Each reply goes to the repository’s own parser, with the configured separator and the limit on moves per turn."],
    ["Normalizing in groups", "Each game’s total reward, with any format penalty, is normalized within its group of games from the same start: the group mean is subtracted and the result divided by the group’s spread. A group whose rewards are all equal gets 0, so it teaches nothing."],
    ["A shorter history, its own idea", "The small model copies whatever earlier answer it sees, broken ones included. So a prompt keeps the previous turn only when that turn produced valid moves, and otherwise shows the current view alone. In its own offline Sokoban runs, this raised well-formed answers on later turns from about 45% to 75%, and solved games from 5.5% to 9.6%."],
  ]},
  "Approximate-MIPS Query Stopping": {m:'opus', steps:[
    ["Keeping the budget", "The rule first keeps the original limit: once the query has checked its allowed number of candidate vectors, it stops at once. Every other test only ends the probing earlier."],
    ["From score to angle", "A stored vector beats the current k-th best result, relaxed by the approximation ratio c, only if its inner product exceeds the k-th best score divided by c. Dividing by the partition’s largest norm turns this into an angle to the query. If no angle qualifies, the partition stops at once."],
    ["The probability test", "The paper’s lookup table gives, for an angle and a bucket score, the chance that one hash table has already probed a vector at that angle. With L tables the chance becomes one minus the miss chance to the power L. The rule stops once this reaches 90%."],
    ["Reading the table lazily", "It reads the table’s score columns from disk only as probing reaches them, and remembers, for each angle, the first column that passes. Values very close to the threshold get the exact formula. The angle is cached while the k-th best score stays the same."],
    ["Settled partitions, its own idea", "If k results already score at least the partition’s largest possible inner product, no unseen vector there, or in any partition of shorter vectors, can enter the answer. The rule then stops. It checks this only after 1, 2, 4, 8 and later powers-of-two probes, to keep the check cheap."],
  ]},
  "BABBLE": {m:'opus', steps:[
    ["Crossing the automaton", "The crossing pairs every rule of the e-graph’s tree automaton with every rule of the same automaton. Two rules with the same number of children give one paired rule, which maps pairs of child states to a pair of output states."],
    ["Anti-unifying pairs of e-classes", "A depth-first search computes, once per pair of e-classes, the set of patterns common to both. Nodes with the same operation combine every pattern of each child pair into a larger pattern, within the limit on holes. Where the two classes differ, the pair becomes a hole."],
    ["Shared patterns", "All patterns live in one table that stores each distinct subpattern once, keyed with a fast integer hash. Pairs of e-classes that can never occur together are skipped. On e-graphs without cycles, the order of the visits cannot change the result, so the search only builds patterns for pairs that can produce one."],
    ["Normalizing candidates", "Each pattern’s holes are renamed to variables in order of first appearance, and repeats are dropped. Patterns without holes are dropped unless constants may be learned, and so are patterns with more holes than allowed."],
    ["Pruning equivalent candidates", "When equations make the e-graph cyclic, many candidates match exactly the same places. For candidates with at most two holes, it matches each one against every e-class and keeps only the smallest candidate of each group, as the later deduplication step would."],
  ]},
  "BBQ": {m:'opus', steps:[
    ["Two stages per level", "Each bitmap level becomes two pipeline stages, so it adds one cycle of latency. The first picks the child: the priority’s digit for an enqueue, the first or last set bit of the level’s row for a dequeue. The second decides whether that child becomes empty and writes the updated row."],
    ["Finding the set bit fast", "A prefix OR that doubles its reach at each step marks the lowest or highest set bit as a single 1. Fixed bit masks then turn that bit into its index. Both steps use shallow logic, which keeps the clock fast."],
    ["Emptiness from flags", "Each level keeps a flag per child that says whether its counter equals one, in registers for levels with up to 4,096 counters. A dequeue then knows at once whether it empties the child, without waiting on a memory read. Counters in memory are written back one stage later."],
    ["Back-to-back operations", "Each level loads the row one stage early and forwards newer updates into it. Whether the operation just ahead empties its child is only known during the current cycle. So the level computes both possible answers, with and without that child, and picks one at the end of the cycle."],
  ]},
  "Binary Fuse Filter Construction and Lookup": {m:'opus', steps:[
    ["Hash and layout", "Each key is multiplied by a random odd seed, and the halves of the 128-bit product are folded together. The high half picks the first slot, and the second and third slots lie in the next two segments. The fingerprint comes from independent bits of the same product, and the filter stores fewer fields, which saves memory."],
    ["Counting and peeling", "Each attempt records, for every slot, how many keys use it and the XOR of their hashes. A slot with one key identifies that key, which is then removed from its other two slots. A queue records the order in which keys are peeled, and is filled without branches so the processor can overlap the work."],
    ["Segment by segment", "Large key sets are first grouped by the segment of their first slot. A segment is complete once its group has been added, so peeling runs right behind the adding, segment by segment, while the data is still in cache. Incomplete segments carry a large extra count so they cannot look peelable."],
    ["Assigning fingerprints", "In reverse peeling order, each key’s peeled slot gets the value that makes the XOR of its three slots equal its fingerprint. The key’s other two slots are already final at that point. Large tables prefetch the next keys’ slots ahead of use."],
    ["Repeated keys", "Repeated keys can never be peeled, but checking for them is slow. It checks only when a failed attempt leaves a slot whose two keys have equal hashes, or after a few failures. It then sorts the keys, removes duplicates and tries a new seed."],
  ]},
  "CKKS noise": {m:'opus', steps:[
    ["A lighter noisy value", "A noisy value keeps the clear message apart from its noise, in ordinary double-precision complex numbers instead of arbitrary precision. The noise is split into three terms, attached to 1, the secret s and s². Decryption adds the noise terms, weighted by those powers and divided by the scale, to the message."],
    ["Multiplying noise", "A product forms every cross term: each message times the other value’s noise, plus noise times noise. The term attached to s² comes from the two s terms, as in a real ciphertext product."],
    ["Key switching", "Relinearization moves the s² term back onto s. It multiplies that term by s², adds the noise of each digit of the key-switching decomposition divided by the special modulus, and adds the rounding of the final division. Digits too small to matter beside that rounding are skipped."],
    ["Rescaling and division", "Rescaling divides every noise term by the last prime of the modulus chain and adds fresh rounding noise, of variance one twelfth per ring coefficient, mapped onto the slots. Division by the special modulus works the same way. Bootstrapping’s transforms also run in double precision."],
    ["Many noise replicas, its own idea", "The estimator carries 16 independent copies of the noise, each with its own sampled secret key. When it decrypts, it keeps the copy whose mean and spread of per-slot precision lie closest to the average over all copies, so that one unlucky sample does not skew the prediction."],
  ]},
  "Clusterwise SpGEMM": {m:'opus', steps:[
    ["Building the clustered format", "For each group of eight consecutive rows, it merges the rows’ sorted column lists into one ascending list. Each column id keeps eight values, one per row, with zero where a row has no entry. A first pass counts list sizes and a second fills them; groups with unsorted rows fall back to sorting."],
    ["Counting output columns", "A first multiply pass counts the distinct output columns of every group, so the output is allocated exactly. It uses a growing hash table, or, when the matrix has a moderate number of columns, a bitmap with one bit per column."],
    ["Eight rows at once", "For each column id k of a group, it reads row k of the second matrix once. Each entry’s product with the eight values is added into a per-group hash table with one 512-bit vector instruction where the processor supports it. It keeps the human reference’s order of additions and rounding, so results match it."],
    ["Streaming the output", "A group accumulates in scratch memory that stays in cache, then is copied to the output with stores that bypass the cache. Large arrays use huge memory pages and are reused across calls."],
    ["Balancing the threads", "Each group’s work is estimated by the entries of the second matrix it will read. Heavy groups run first, largest first, each as its own unit; the rest run in contiguous runs of roughly equal work. When exact zeros are common, columns that are zero in all eight rows are dropped from the output."],
  ]},
  "dbscan kernel": {m:'opus', steps:[
    ["Spatial ordering", "Points are assigned to the nearest of a set of pivot points and sorted by cell. Each cell is padded to whole tiles of points and ordered along a fixed direction inside, so every tile covers a compact slab of space."],
    ["Pruning tile pairs", "Each tile gets a bounding sphere. Only pairs of tiles whose spheres come within the radius eps are examined; all other pairs cannot hold neighbors and are skipped."],
    ["Exact neighbor tests", "Every distance decision reproduces the plain version’s 32-bit arithmetic bit for bit. A fast tensor-core pass decides the pairs that lie clearly inside or outside eps, and pairs near the boundary are rechecked with the exact arithmetic. The result is a neighbor count per point and packed neighbor bits."],
    ["Linking core points", "Within a tile, core points spread the smallest label to their linked neighbors until nothing changes. Across tiles, a lock-free union-find joins the pieces; when both tiles are uniform, one union suffices. Each cluster takes the smallest point index it can reach."],
    ["Borders, noise and launches", "A non-core point next to a core point takes the smallest neighboring core label; the rest are noise. Cluster ids are the ranks of the labels. For moderate sizes, the whole pipeline is replayed as one recorded GPU graph, which removes kernel-launch overhead."],
  ]},
  "Differentiable Population Statistics": {m:'opus', steps:[
    ["Three ways to keep the population", "The queue supports the three estimators the training setups use: a ring buffer of the latest generated features, the same ring with running sums of the features and their outer products, and moving averages of the first and second moments, started from a warm start of generated images."],
    ["Gradients through the batch only", "To form the statistics, it combines the stored state, read without gradients, with the current batch, which keeps its graph. With running sums, it subtracts the rows about to be evicted and adds the batch’s sums; the mean and covariance follow from these moments."],
    ["Updating without old graphs", "After each step, it writes the detached batch over the oldest rows and adjusts the running sums by the difference, or moves the averages toward the batch. No computation graph outlives the step, and the state sits in buffers that save and load with the model."],
    ["Double precision", "All statistics are formed in 64-bit floats. Its own comments give the reason: in single precision, the covariance of a 50,000-row population loses its small eigenvalues, and rounding noise swamps the gradient that reaches the batch."],
    ["A tracking window, its own idea", "It argues that a long window, or a slowly decaying average, lags the generator, which then overshoots. So a long window gives way to an exponential window started from the warm start, holding about 1.5 times as many features as the feature dimension; for a ring buffer, repeated rows stand in for these weights."],
  ]},
  "Diffusion Policy": {m:'opus', steps:[
    ["Five network calls", "Instead of the human reference’s 100 denoising steps, the loop calls the fixed network only five times, at noise levels 95, 70, 45, 22 and 5 of its training schedule. Sampling starts from Gaussian noise at the first level."],
    ["A clean guess at each level", "At each level, the network’s noise estimate gives a guess of the clean move sequence, clipped to the range the scheduler allows. Before each call, the parts of the sequence fixed by the observations are written back in."],
    ["Second-order jumps", "Between levels, it follows the deterministic probability-flow path, measured in log signal-to-noise ratio, with the multistep DPM-Solver++ update. Each jump combines the current and the previous clean guesses, which makes it second order."],
    ["A free correction", "On reaching a new level, it corrects the point it landed on with the UniPC corrector, which uses the clean guess just made there, so the correction costs no extra network call. The last clean guess becomes the plan."],
  ]},
  "DyT": {m:'opus', steps:[
    ["The layer", "Each DyT layer holds a learned scalar α, started at 0.5, and per-channel γ and β, started at one and zero. It computes γ · tanh(αx) + β, and broadcasts γ and β over the spatial axes when channels come first."],
    ["The conversion", "It walks the model tree and replaces every LayerNorm with a DyT layer of the same shape, noting whether that site normalizes the last axis or the channel axis. Children are converted recursively, so no LayerNorm is left."],
    ["A fused kernel that matches exactly", "On the GPU in single precision, the forward and the backward pass each run as one small kernel compiled at run time. It rounds every multiply and add separately, as the plain formula does, so outputs and gradients match the human reference’s layer bit for bit."],
    ["Replaying the block stack, its own idea", "For a Vision Transformer, the converted stack of blocks records its kernels once into CUDA graphs, for the forward and the backward pass, and replays them on later steps. This removes the cost of launching hundreds of small kernels without changing any value; any change to modules, parameters or settings falls back to normal execution."],
  ]},
  "egg": {m:'opus', steps:[
    ["Repairing pending parents", "For each pending node, it replaces every child class with its canonical representative and reinserts the node in the hash-cons memo. If the memo already maps that node to another class, the two classes are congruent, so it unions them, which can queue more parents."],
    ["Propagating analysis data", "For each class whose analysis is pending, it recomputes the node’s data and merges it into the class’s data. If the data changed, the class’s parents go back on the queue and the analysis can modify the class. Both loops repeat until neither queue has work."],
    ["Canonicalizing every class", "It then visits every class once: it rewrites each node’s children to canonical ids, sorts the nodes and removes duplicates. It also rebuilds the index from each operator to the classes that contain it, which pattern matching uses."],
    ["Checking the memo", "In debug builds, a check confirms that every node appears in the memo under its own canonical class, and that no two classes hold the same node. The rebuild reports how many unions it made."],
  ]},
  "Exact Sequence Alignment": {m:'opus', steps:[
    ["A banded pass with checkpoints", "Since the exact distance is known, one forward pass only needs the band of rows that a path of that cost can reach. It runs Myers’ bit-parallel algorithm on blocks of 64 rows and saves the state at sparse checkpoint columns."],
    ["Strips traced right to left", "Starting from the end cell, it resolves the strips between checkpoints from right to left. A narrow strip is recomputed from its checkpoint with full storage, within a small memory budget, and traced back directly, skipping runs of matches along the diagonal."],
    ["Splitting wide strips", "For a wide strip, a short backward pass from the exit cell meets the checkpoint column. The row where the forward and backward costs add up to the known cost is where an optimal path enters, and the part in between is solved recursively, as in Hirschberg’s method."],
    ["A shortcut for similar pairs", "When the cost is small compared with the length, it uses a diagonal-transition search instead: for each cost, it tracks how far each diagonal can run along matches, then traces back from the end cell."],
    ["Columns in parallel", "The bit-parallel kernel runs as a skewed wavefront over several neighboring columns at once, so that their carry chains run side by side in AVX-512 or AVX2 vector registers when the processor has them."],
  ]},
  "FARGO paper-aligned query core": {m:'opus', steps:[
    ["Distances from flip costs", "In each table, flipping a bit costs the squared projection of the query on that bit. A bucket’s distance is the sum of the costs of the bits where it differs from the query’s own key, so the query’s own bucket in every table comes first, at 0."],
    ["A heap for the first probes", "Early buckets come from a small heap over all tables. It grows sets of flipped bits by shift and expand moves over each table’s sorted costs, so every bucket appears once, in ascending distance, with ties going to the lower table."],
    ["Sorted batches for deep probes", "Deeper probing switches to batches: each table’s sorted costs are split into a low and a high half with precomputed sorted subset sums, and every bucket below the next threshold is emitted at once, then sorted. Both phases compute the same sum, so the handoff is exact."],
    ["The stop rule", "From the k-th best inner product so far, the approximation ratio and the bound on the largest inner product, it reads the paper’s calibration table. It stops when the k-th result reaches the bound, or when the chance that more probes across all tables help falls below 0.1. For fixed inputs this is a cutoff distance, cached until they change."],
  ]},
  "Glenside": {m:'opus', steps:[
    ["A fact for every operator", "For each kind of node, the analysis builds its fact from its children’s facts: the outer and item shapes of an access pattern, the output size of windows and pooling, and broadcast shapes. A Cartesian product, for example, joins the outer shapes and pairs the items."],
    ["Marking accelerator use", "Systolic-array nodes, their blocked and convolution forms, and explicit accelerator calls get the shape of the product they compute and are marked as containing accelerator use. Every parent inherits the mark, so a class knows whether an accelerator form lies inside."],
    ["Merging facts", "When two classes join, the merge checks that both facts cover the same number of elements. A settled shape wins over an unsettled one, accelerator use is kept if either side has it, and the merge reports exactly which side changed, so the e-graph revisits only what moved."],
    ["Rewrites to matrix form, its own idea", "It also changes two rewrites. A dot product of a Cartesian product is rebuilt over flattened operands, so the accelerator rule sees a plain matrix product. A concatenation of two such dot products becomes one dot product of block operands, zero-padded so the extra products add nothing. Each rule skips classes it has already rewritten."],
  ]},
  "Hygra": {m:'opus', steps:[
    ["Peeling level by level", "Each level is the smallest live degree among the remaining vertices. Every vertex at that degree leaves and takes the level as its core number. A hyperedge dies with its first removed member, and each of its other members loses one from its degree; members that fall to the level leave in the next round of the same level."],
    ["Three ways to run a round", "Push: each live hyperedge that touches the leaving vertices is handled once, by its lowest-numbered leaving member, which decrements the other members. Pull: the leaving vertices mark their hyperedges dead, and every remaining vertex recounts its live hyperedges. Fix: when few vertices remain, only they are recounted, until none drops."],
    ["A cost model picks the mode", "Before each round, it estimates the work of each mode from the frontier size, the level, the average vertex degree and the average hyperedge size, and runs the cheapest. Push suits small frontiers and pull suits huge ones. When the frontier holds every remaining vertex, they all take the current level at once."],
    ["Ownership instead of atomics", "Each thread owns aligned blocks of 64 vertices and of the dead-hyperedge bitmap. In parallel push rounds, threads send kills and decrements as messages to the owner, so no atomic operation touches the hot arrays. One array holds the live degrees and, for removed vertices, their final core numbers."],
  ]},
  "ivf_pq kernel": {m:'opus', steps:[
    ["Choosing the lists to probe", "Tensor cores compute approximate query-to-centroid distances in bfloat16, with a rigorous bound on their error. Lists that are clearly inside or outside the nearest set are settled at once. Only the centroids near the boundary are recomputed exactly; the probes are then ordered by distance and centroid id."],
    ["Scanning list by list", "It groups the query–list pairs by list, so each list’s codes are decoded once and compared with every query that probes it, as one tensor-core matrix product. The approximate distance is the query-to-centroid distance plus terms of the decoded residual."],
    ["A safe shortlist, then exact top-k", "A streaming filter per query keeps a small superset that provably contains the true k best, with the error bound as its margin. It rescores this shortlist with the plain search’s table-lookup arithmetic and sorts it by distance, then by index."],
    ["Exact output, its own idea", "Every fast pass is approximate but carries an error bound, and anything near a boundary is recomputed with the plain search’s own arithmetic and order of summation. So the output matches the slow search exactly: the same lists, float32 distances and tie-breaking. Tiny query batches stay on the plain path, where the matrix library rounds differently."],
  ]},
  "KAMI": {m:'opus', steps:[
    ["Streaming the inputs", "With one small, independent multiplication per block, the batch is bound by memory bandwidth, so the kernel is built to keep memory busy. It copies A and B into shared memory with asynchronous 16-byte copies: all of K at once when they fit in 48 KiB, otherwise in a multi-stage pipeline over K."],
    ["Fusing the old C", "Each thread loads its part of C straight into its accumulator registers and scales it by one half before the products start, so the read of C overlaps the traffic of A and B. Results leave the accumulators as 16-byte streaming stores."],
    ["Double-precision tensor cores", "The products use the FP64 tensor-core instruction on 16×8×8 fragments. At compile time, it lays the warps over the output fragments so that as many warps as divide the grid evenly take part, with the fewest shared-memory loads per product."],
    ["Conflict-free shared loads", "Inside each 8-wide step of K, it remaps the two K positions a thread needs onto adjacent ones, in both A and B. The sum is unchanged, but each pair becomes one 16-byte load, and a swizzle of the rows keeps simultaneous reads on different memory banks."],
    ["Reverse launch order, its own idea", "Blocks work through the batch from the end: block b takes the b-th slice from the last. The first wave of blocks thus starts on the most recently written end of the batch, which is still in cache. Every slice is still computed exactly once."],
  ]},
  "kmeans kernel": {m:'opus', steps:[
    ["Three kernels per step", "A preparation kernel pads the centers, computes their squared norms and clears the sum buffers. The main kernel assigns every point and accumulates the cluster sums. A final kernel divides each sum by its count; an empty cluster keeps its old center."],
    ["Distances on tensor cores", "For each block of 128 points, the main kernel computes the distances to one tile of centers after another as a bfloat16 matrix product with float32 accumulation, and never stores the full point-by-center table. Short feature vectors stay in registers; long ones stream in chunks, with an exact running minimum per row."],
    ["Overlapped cluster sums", "Each point adds its coordinates and a count to its center’s sum with atomic additions. With few centers, blocks write to private copies of the sums to spread contention. The additions for one block of points are deferred and spread over the center-tile loop of the next block, so they overlap the matrix work."],
    ["Packed tile index, its own idea", "For short vectors, it writes the center tile’s index into the lowest mantissa bits of each distance. A plain element-wise minimum over the tiles then carries the winning tile along with the value, without branches. With more than 16 tiles, it first adds the point’s own norm, so the bits it gives up stay small relative to the true distance."],
  ]},
  "knn kernel": {m:'opus', steps:[
    ["A tensor-core pass that keeps two", "A tiled bfloat16 matrix product gives approximate squared distances. For each query and each group of 128 or 256 database rows, it keeps only the two smallest values, or four for long vectors and large k, packed with the row’s position in the group. The full distance table is never stored."],
    ["A bound on the k-th distance", "Per query, it takes minima over interleaved sets of groups to bound the k-th distance from above, widened by a rigorous float32 error bound. Groups whose minimum falls below the bound contribute their best row; groups whose second value does too are rescanned in full."],
    ["Exact rescoring", "The surviving candidates get their distances recomputed exactly in double precision. It sorts them by packed keys of distance and position and returns the k smallest in ascending order, so the reported distances match a direct recomputation."],
    ["Low overhead per call", "All kernels run without host synchronization, and buffers are reused across calls. A launch plan recorded for a known shape is replayed directly, which skips the per-kernel Python dispatch. Tiny or unusual problems fall back to an exact double-precision search in chunks."],
    ["Exact integer path, its own idea", "SIFT descriptors are whole numbers from 0 to 255. When every value is one, it shifts them by −128 into 8-bit integers and computes the dot products in 32-bit integers, which are exact, so the first pass carries no rounding error. Other data takes the bfloat16 path."],
  ]},
  "LeJEPA": {m:'opus', steps:[
    ["Alignment in a whitened metric", "The alignment loss measures the difference between the two views in the metric of the batch covariance, which equals the squared error between whitened views. Because it is invariant to affine maps of the outputs, it cannot trade scale against alignment."],
    ["SIGReg in three parts", "An unbiased moment penalty, built from two independent half-batches, sets the mean to zero and the covariance to the identity. An Epps–Pulley test compares the characteristic function of the whitened outputs with a Gaussian’s along eight fixed directions. A radial-tail test against a chi-square distribution keeps the rarely seen outer region from shrinking."],
    ["Whitening and InfoNCE", "The whitening loss is the squared distance of the batch mean and covariance from those of a standard Gaussian. The plain InfoNCE loss uses a Gaussian kernel between the two views of each row. No loss keeps state between calls."],
    ["A Gaussian critic, its own idea", "The alignment loss also carries a small InfoNCE term whose critic is the exact Gaussian log-density of one view given the other, from the known view correlation. Its optimum is the same rotation of the state, and its in-batch repulsion against all other rows of both views sharpens the learned geometry."],
  ]},
  "MetaOpt": {m:'opus', steps:[
    ["An exact private scorer", "It replays every candidate with its own small simplex solver for the path-form maximum-flow program, after a presolve that drops links that can never bind. The gap is the optimal throughput minus the better of POP and pinning. Cached POP results and cheap upper bounds skip the optimal solve when a candidate cannot beat the current best."],
    ["Iterated local search", "From a start vector it descends by first improvement in random order: change one pair’s level, or move a nonzero demand to an empty pair; deeper descents also try changes to two pairs at once. Every move keeps at most nine nonzero demands and the pinned demands within link capacity."],
    ["Threads and an elite pool", "Up to four threads search for three seconds of wall time. They share the best vector so far and a pool of 24 good, distinct vectors. Each round perturbs one to four pairs of a pool vector and descends again; every tenth round restarts from a random vector."],
    ["A careful final pick", "Equal-best vectors can replay to values that differ in the last bits. Among the tied best vectors, it keeps the one that the task’s exact oracle scores highest, and falls back to its own replay’s choice if the oracle fails."],
    ["Seeds from cuts, its own idea", "Before the local search, it enumerates small sets of links that cut the network. A cut makes a strong seed when demands of 18 must all cross it while many small pinned demands crowd it too: pinning then loses throughput that the optimum can recover by rerouting. Each thread scores its share of seeds and descends from the best."],
  ]},
  "MuJoCo Playground": {m:'opus', steps:[
    ["PPO from the library’s parts", "It keeps Brax’s PPO loss, policy and value networks and observation normalizer, but drops the library’s fixed training loop of 200 million steps. In its place it writes its own loop, which collects rollouts from 8,192 simulated robots and runs the PPO updates."],
    ["The shipped task, unchanged", "Training uses the authors’ flat-ground environment, reward and domain randomization. Pushes and a mix of environment groups are built in as options, but the chosen setting leaves them off and trains on flat ground only."],
    ["Annealing toward the end", "Over the last 60% of the budget, the learning rate and the entropy bonus fall linearly to a tenth of their starting values. The final policy thus settles instead of being a snapshot of noisy updates at a constant step size."],
    ["Using the deadline, its own idea", "Training runs in short compiled chunks. The loop times each chunk and stops when the next one would no longer finish a safety margin before the deadline, and it skips evaluation rollouts. In its own estimate, this gives about 360 million environment steps instead of 200 million."],
  ]},
  "Nano World Model": {m:'opus', steps:[
    ["Three schedules, one rule", "Each frame starts denoising a fixed number of rows after the one before it. With no lag all frames move together; a lag of one step gives the pyramid; a lag of the whole schedule gives the sequential mode. Row indices then map to evenly spaced diffusion timesteps, with a separate value for clean."],
    ["A per-frame DDIM update", "Each row-to-row move calls the model once for all frames. From its output the update estimates every clean frame, whether the model predicts noise, velocity or the clean frame, and moves each frame to its next noise level by a DDIM step. Frames whose level does not drop stay unchanged."],
    ["A lightly noised history", "The real context frame is not shown perfectly clean. The sampler noises it to a fixed low level before each run and shows it to the model at that level in every row."],
    ["Sixteen samples, averaged, its own idea", "For each clip it runs 16 independent samplers of 50 steps and averages their latent videos. It then re-noises the average slightly and denoises it once more, frame by frame, in 10 steps, so the prediction leans toward what most samples agree on."],
  ]},
  "OCR Distillation Trajectory Sampling": {m:'opus', steps:[
    ["One guided call per step", "The sampler starts from noise in single precision and asks the student once per step for a classifier-free-guided velocity. It hands that velocity to the step function and records every state, every log-probability and every mean, so replay sees exactly the inputs that sampling saw."],
    ["A reverse-SDE Gaussian step", "The step takes one Euler–Maruyama step of the reverse-time stochastic equation that keeps the flow’s marginals, from the current noise level to the next. The mean follows from the state and the velocity; the log-probability is the Gaussian density, computed in double precision. Given a recorded next state, it scores it without drawing new noise."],
    ["A second-order solver without noise", "When the noise level is zero, as for evaluation images, the sampler integrates the deterministic flow with the two-step DPM-Solver++ method. It passes the result to the step as an equivalent velocity, so every recorded state is still the step’s mean."],
    ["Capped early noise, its own idea", "Near pure noise the usual Flow-GRPO noise is so wide that a step says little about the velocity. It caps the noise so every step keeps a minimum weight in the distillation loss. In its own analysis, the uncapped early steps, where the text teacher differs most, carry hundreds of times less signal than the last one."],
  ]},
  "Parallel DBSCAN": {m:'opus', steps:[
    ["Lock-free union-find", "Each core cell starts as its own component. Threads merge components with atomic compare-and-swap, always linking a root to the smaller index, so no locks are needed and each component’s id is its smallest cell. Fewer than 48 core cells are checked pair by pair on one thread."],
    ["Nearest neighbors first", "Candidate cell pairs fall into three phases. Cells that differ by one in a single coordinate come first, found by merging the sorted list of cells with a shifted copy of itself. Diagonal neighbors follow, then cells two or more apart in some coordinate but still within reach of ε."],
    ["Exact tests that stop early", "A pair of cells first compares one representative core point from each, then scans a few point pairs directly. Only if both fail does it compare the bounding boxes of the core points, keep the points close to the other cell’s box and check those exhaustively, with the reference’s own arithmetic so the result matches exactly."],
    ["Skipping settled subtrees, its own idea", "Diagonal and far candidates come from a trie over cell coordinates. Before each phase, every trie node records the component its whole subtree shares, and a subtree already in the querying cell’s component is skipped. Once a single component remains, the search stops."],
  ]},
  "ParButterfly": {m:'opus', steps:[
    ["A strategy per graph", "It first counts the wedges each side would produce as centers and takes the side with fewer. On graphs with near-uniform degrees it orders vertices by side; on skewed graphs it also counts the wedges a degree order would process and picks the cheaper of the two by a simple cost model."],
    ["Counting from the larger endpoint", "In side order, each endpoint is handled on its own. For every neighbor, the smaller endpoints form a prefix of that neighbor’s sorted list, each one a wedge. A small per-thread counter collects the shared neighbors of each pair, and a pair sharing c neighbors adds c(c − 1)/2 butterflies."],
    ["Vectorized prefix scans", "Neighbor lists are copied once into fixed-width slots of 16-bit ids padded with a sentinel, so vector instructions find and copy a prefix without branches. A vector pass then sums the squared counters and clears them. Each thread times two forms of the counting loop on its first chunks and keeps the faster."],
    ["Degree priority for skewed graphs", "On skewed graphs vertices are ranked by degree, and each butterfly is counted once from its highest-ranked vertex, so the few heavy hubs seldom serve as wedge centers. Lists sorted by rank make the lower-ranked neighbors a prefix, and low-degree vertices use 8-bit counters."],
  ]},
  "Persistent Treap Update Engine": {m:'opus', steps:[
    ["Top-down path copying", "Insert and delete copy the path from the root to the change in one downward pass. Every ancestor gains or loses one key, so its subtree count is written once, when the copy is made. A deletion first finds its key, so a missing key creates no nodes."],
    ["Units owned by workers", "Nodes with priority above a threshold form the top of the treap, and each subtree below them, a unit, holds the keys between two consecutive top keys. The submitting thread routes each update to the worker that owns its unit, and workers copy paths inside their units independently."],
    ["A linker publishes versions", "A linker thread walks the versions in order, copies the short top path of each update, attaches the worker’s new unit root and publishes the version. Updates that add or remove a top key split or merge units, with the work shared between worker and linker, so readers only ever see whole trees."],
    ["Memory carved in bulk", "Nodes are never freed, so path copies come from large regions by a bump allocator. A helper thread touches the next pages ahead of the linker, and once all requests are in, unused tails are returned so they never count toward peak memory."],
    ["Promoting hot ranges, its own idea", "It samples recent update keys. When 90% fall in a narrow range, the linker raises the top node over that range to the root by boosting its priority. Updates there then stop copying the ancestors on the other side; the set of keys is unchanged."],
  ]},
  "Polar Express": {m:'opus', steps:[
    ["The best polynomial by Remez", "For an interval, it finds the odd polynomial with the smallest worst-case distance from 1 by the Remez exchange. It solves for coefficients whose error alternates in sign on a set of reference points, moves the references to the error’s extrema, refined by Newton steps, and repeats until they stop moving."],
    ["A greedy schedule", "Each polynomial is the best one for the interval that the previous ones leave around 1. The whole composition is computed in double precision, so the safety margins the paper adds for low precision are not needed."],
    ["Estimating the spectrum", "The iteration runs in double precision on the matrix scaled by its Frobenius norm. A few products, reused by the first step, give power sums of the singular values; a geometric spectrum fitted to them sets the scale and the smallest singular value when it matches a further sum, and otherwise a rigorous bound does."],
    ["Higher degrees, its own idea", "A fifth-degree step costs three matrix products, and it spends that budget differently. For each step count it uses a fixed mix of degrees up to 17, a degree-17 polynomial taking five products through a nested evaluation scheme. Their schedules are tabulated over a fine grid of lower bounds when the module loads."],
  ]},
  "Polygon": {m:'opus', steps:[
    ["Growing the row bound", "It looks for a counterexample with at most one row per table, then two, and so on up to the limit. Each bound is a fresh, smaller formula. A bound that is refuted or left undecided moves the search on to the next."],
    ["Small tables, then conflicts", "Within one bound, each table starts with few live rows and the rest assumed deleted, given to Z3 as assumptions. On unsat, the unsat core names the tables whose sizes took part. It records this conflict and lets only those tables grow by one row."],
    ["Soft preferences, parallel workers", "Preferences such as exact integer division and dates inside the database’s range stay until a conflict blames them and growing cannot help. Several schedules run at once, each with its own Z3. They share the size settings already refuted, and the first model found stops the rest."],
    ["Splitting query sets", "To split a set of queries into two equal groups, it asks for a database on which query 0 anchors the first group, every query of the second group gives one shared result, and each group holds exactly half. It first tries the stronger case where those results have at most one row."],
    ["Solver in the same process", "Formulas are written out as SMT-LIB text and loaded into Z3 inside the same process. Strings get integer codes that keep their order, approximating the database’s case- and accent-insensitive comparison."],
  ]},
  "QuarterPIR": {m:'opus', steps:[
    ["Dropping superblock keys", "The request is the authors’ request with the keys of the first superblocks removed and the offsets bit-packed. The client stores the parities those keys would let the server compute. Client memory bounds how many keys it drops: all four for the small database, one of 32 for the large one."],
    ["Replaying the reference client", "The client makes exactly the reference client’s choices: the same hints, random coins and refresh order. So its request stays a fixed public function of the authors’ request. It stores each hint as one parity per dropped superblock, plus one for all the others."],
    ["One candidate per superblock", "For each superblock, the server XORs the entries at the offsets that fall in it with the key-defined parities of the other kept superblocks. It returns these candidates and the kept parities. The client takes the candidate for the entry’s superblock and XORs it with its stored parity."],
    ["Fast setup and lookup", "Setup streams the database in cache-sized parts while three workers add the entries to every hint’s parity. A table built during setup narrows each lookup’s search for the hint that holds the entry, so the client checks only a few hints. AES keys are expanded four at a time."],
  ]},
  "RegionsSort": {m:'opus', steps:[
    ["Matching edges into swaps", "The basic step pairs a range of this bucket’s keys held elsewhere with a range of foreign keys inside this bucket’s interval. It swaps their leading parts, as long as the shorter range, and moves both ranges past the swapped part."],
    ["Completing one bucket at a time", "Planning a bucket pairs all of its keys held elsewhere with all misplaced slots of its own interval, so that bucket ends up complete. Each misplaced range belongs to whichever of its two buckets is planned first."],
    ["Two-way swaps first", "It first takes pairs where the displaced keys belong to the other range’s bucket, since both sides then land home in one swap. It fills the remaining slots in address order, and fuses swaps that continue each other on both sides into one."],
    ["Deferred graph updates", "The ranges that the swaps leave in other buckets’ intervals are recorded only after the caller has started sorting the finished bucket. They go into small per-bucket lists of chunks, and large scratch buffers are freed, which keeps the extra memory low."],
  ]},
  "Residual State Advancement": {m:'opus', steps:[
    ["One small table per branch", "Every table that sales joins, together with all tables reachable from it, forms a branch whose joins are many-to-one. It joins each branch once into a small side table with one row per join key that appears in sales, never a table the size of sales."],
    ["A slim residual table", "The residuals live in a slim table that holds only the residual and the sales table’s join keys. The first tree reads it through a view, and each residual update writes the next one."],
    ["One pass per tree", "After a tree, each sales row joins its keys to the side tables, where every split test is precomputed per key as bits. One query then subtracts the learning rate times the leaf value. If the tree splits on only one branch, it looks up each key’s prediction directly."],
    ["Sums reused across nodes and trees", "Split statistics come from per-key residual sums kept beside each branch’s features. For each split it aggregates only the smaller child and derives the sibling by subtraction. When a tree splits on one branch only, the next tree’s starting sums follow from the previous ones without scanning sales."],
  ]},
  "Robust Image Tokenizer": {m:'opus', steps:[
    ["Hiding only a few patches", "In training it hides a random number of patches per batch, at most a tenth of the share the configured mask ratio allows. Random scores shuffle the positions, the first ones stay visible, and the order is kept to undo the shuffle. An explicitly requested ratio is used exactly."],
    ["Light noise on the latents", "Each sample’s latents are mixed with Gaussian noise scaled by gamma, with a weight drawn per sample between 0.5% and 5%. The run is short and evaluation is clean, so it keeps training close to plain reconstruction."],
    ["Restoring the order", "The decoder appends its learned mask token once for every hidden position and then undoes the shuffle. The sequence is back in image order before the positional embeddings are added."],
    ["Faster model setup, its own idea", "Building the model draws about 343 million random numbers one at a time. It reproduces the same random stream faster: Mersenne Twister jump-ahead skips the draws that get overwritten, and the rest are made in parallel chunks. Weights and generator state come out bit-identical."],
  ]},
  "RUNLTS": {m:'opus', steps:[
    ["Tracking the newest producer", "For each of 33 registers, the 31 general ones, the stack pointer and the flags, it records which instruction last claimed it at decode. An executed value is stored only if it comes from that newest claimant. A late, older instruction is ignored, and the register stays unusable until its newest producer runs."],
    ["Picking recent registers", "At each prediction it takes the six registers whose usable values come from the most recent producers. It hashes each of them with the branch address into one shared table of small signed counters."],
    ["Exact and coarse lookups", "Each register is looked up twice: once with its exact value, and once with a coarse class made of the value’s magnitude, its sign and its two low bits. The coarse lookup lets nearby values share what they learn."],
    ["Joining the corrector’s vote", "The counters’ sum, with saturated counters counted three times, is added to the statistical corrector’s sum. Each of the two lookup groups has a usefulness weight that doubles its vote, trained like the corrector’s other groups. The counters move toward the outcome when the branch resolves."],
  ]},
  "ScaleGPM": {m:'opus', steps:[
    ["Two runtime models", "It predicts each engine’s running time with its own formula. Sampling’s time grows with the number of samples, the average degree and the graph size. Sparsification’s time grows with the vertices and edges, divided by powers of the number of colors, with triangle terms added for 4-cliques."],
    ["Fitted to measured timings", "It fits the coefficients to its own median-of-three timings of the unmodified engines, on generated small-world graphs of 50,000 to 180,000 vertices. There is one set per pattern, triangle or 4-clique, and per core count of 1, 2, 4 or 8."],
    ["Picking the smaller estimate", "For each setting it evaluates both formulas and picks the engine with the smaller predicted time. If the colors are missing it picks sampling. If the sample count is missing, or an estimate fails, it picks sparsification."],
    ["Locality from triangles, its own idea", "Small-world graphs with fewer random shortcuts have more triangles and more local neighbor lists, which merge faster. So it infers the share of rewired edges from the triangle count, against a regular lattice of the same degree, and adds a saturating locality penalty to both formulas."],
  ]},
  "SCOPE": {m:'opus', steps:[
    ["Counts from homomorphisms", "It rewrites each pattern’s count at the orbit vertex as a signed sum of rooted homomorphism counts of the pattern’s quotients, by Möbius inversion over vertex partitions. Every quotient is a connected rooted graph of at most five vertices, and homomorphisms are far easier to count than distinct copies."],
    ["One cache for the batch", "Each rooted shape gets a canonical code, so the same shape always gets the same name. The batch keeps every intermediate shape it computes, and equal sub-shapes of different queries, such as a shared wedge or triangle, are computed once and reused."],
    ["Trees by message passing", "A shape splits into its branches at the root, and their counts multiply. A branch below a leaf root is one sparse matrix-vector product. Trees that hang off the shape’s dense core become vertex weights, so only the core needs a special counter."],
    ["A kernel for each core", "Dedicated kernels count the cores: triangles, 4-cycles, diamonds, 4-cliques and five-vertex cores such as the house and the 5-clique. They use degree-ordered adjacency, per-edge triangle counts, local bitsets and, when it fits, a dense table of common-neighbor counts with vectorized inner products. Each batch computes only the roots it needs."],
    ["A fallback for the rest", "A query outside this class, or one whose intermediate counts could overflow 64-bit integers, goes to the original one-query-at-a-time path, so every count stays exact."],
  ]},
  "Seer": {m:'opus', steps:[
    ["Remember the last direction", "Each branch has its own small predictor. It stores the direction the branch took most recently and over how many blocks in a row that direction has held. Before its first observation, it answers unsure."],
    ["Count blocks, not observations", "Observations within one block see nearly the same state, so they count once. The predictor finds block boundaries from the replay order: a block makes all its predictions before any of its observations, so the first prediction after an observation starts a new block."],
    ["Commit only when stable", "It predicts the stored direction only after that direction has held, without a change, across two distinct blocks; until then it answers unsure. When the system asks for a training prediction, it always returns the last direction it saw."],
    ["Keep recurring branches", "The system sweeps away the predictors of rarely seen branches. Once a branch has appeared in two distinct blocks, its predictor marks itself as frequent, so recurring branches keep their history while one-off branches are still swept."],
  ]},
  "Shockwave": {m:'opus', steps:[
    ["A replica of every job", "The policy keeps its own copy of each live job’s training state: batch size, iteration budget and iterations run. It copies the simulator’s rules for batch-size changes, both gradient-noise doubling and Accordion switching, so it can forecast how long each job still needs on its own GPUs."],
    ["Forecast from a priority order", "Given an order of the live jobs, an event-driven model fills the four GPUs in that order, round after round. It charges a 20-second restart cost to a job that did not run in the previous round, and returns every job’s predicted completion time."],
    ["One score for four goals", "Each forecast gets one score that rewards a short makespan and a short average completion time, and penalizes the worst finish-time fairness ratio and the number of unfair jobs. The two fairness terms carry three times their base weight."],
    ["Search over orders", "The search starts from seed orders such as last round’s order, shortest remaining time first and earliest fair deadline at several slack levels. It then swaps and moves jobs while the score improves, for at most 1,500 evaluations per round. The jobs that the best order runs first are this round’s choice."],
    ["Safe defaults", "After 150 seconds of planning time in total, it keeps the previous order. If the planner fails or returns an invalid choice, the round falls back to shortest remaining time first, packed onto the free GPUs."],
  ]},
  "Spectrum": {m:'opus', steps:[
    ["Pages of four items", "The stack stores its items in pages of four, and a page table lists the pages in order. A checkpoint holds the code position and a reference to a page table, so saving a deep stack copies no items."],
    ["Copy on write", "Saving a deep stack shares the live page table in constant time. The live stack copies the table, and then each page, only when it first writes there, so every checkpoint keeps the values it saw. A new checkpoint is registered lazily, on the next change, which keeps saving short."],
    ["Flat copies for shallow stacks", "A stack of at most 63 items is simply copied into a self-contained block. That checkpoint then depends on nothing, and the live stack keeps writing its own pages in place."],
    ["Constant-time restore", "Restoring one of the stack’s own checkpoints swaps its page table back in and raises a reference count, again in constant time. A flat copy is written back item by item, and a checkpoint from another stack is copied in."],
    ["Memory inside the stack space", "Pages and tables come from free lists carved out of the execution state’s own preallocated stack space, and fall back to the heap only when that runs out. A mark-and-sweep pass frees pages that no table refers to, and reads take a direct path while the bottom pages lie contiguously in memory."],
  ]},
  "Structured LWE": {m:'opus', steps:[
    ["Secrets only", "The final submission is a list of secrets, one per solved instance. It contains no attack code, so the methods that found these secrets cannot be read from it."],
    ["144 of 200 instances", "It submits secrets for 144 of the 200 instances: all 20 in six of the ten families, and the first six in each of the other four families."],
    ["Secrets of any value", "In four families, 80 instances in all, every entry of every secret is nonzero, with values up to about 4,000. These match the families whose secret may take any value mod q, and each of them gets an answer."],
    ["Sparse binary and ternary secrets", "In two more families every secret has exactly 14 nonzero entries, binary and ternary in turn from one instance to the next. In the partly answered families the secrets are binary or ternary with 2 to 8 nonzeros, except one family with dense binary secrets, about half ones."],
  ]},
  "SVG-EAR": {m:'opus', steps:[
    ["Clusters with warm starts", "Each sparse step runs k-means on the queries, with 600 clusters, and on the keys, starting from the previous step’s centroids and running one iteration. A stable sort then places each cluster’s tokens next to each other, keeping their raster order inside the cluster."],
    ["Means in one pass", "A fused GPU kernel computes the mean of each contiguous cluster of keys and values, and also its spread, the mean squared distance to that mean. These cluster averages feed both the choice of pairs and the estimate of the skipped ones."],
    ["Picking cluster pairs", "Each query cluster scores the key clusters by a size-weighted softmax of centroid dot products, and keeps the best until a top-p share of the row is covered, with at most a quarter of all key tokens. The code also holds an error-aware score, but its final setting ranks by estimated attention mass."],
    ["Exact pairs plus an estimate", "A block-sparse kernel computes the chosen pairs exactly and returns their log-sum-exp. Every skipped key cluster then enters as its centroid, weighted by its size, with a capped correction of its log-mass for the spread of its keys. The two parts merge into one output."],
    ["Where the time goes", "The first denoising steps, plus 15 more, use full attention with the same kernel as the reference, which keeps the early trajectory aligned with it. The second, unconditional pass of each step reuses the first pass’s clusters and chosen pairs, and any failure falls back to full attention."],
  ]},
  "Yannakakis": {m:'opus', steps:[
    ["Joins as tree edges", "Each binary join becomes an edge between two tables: on each side, the leftmost table that holds the join’s keys. The tables and these edges form a join tree, which any choice of root turns back into a plan."],
    ["Rebuilding a valid plan", "From the chosen root, it builds a left-deep spine and joins each child’s subplan onto it, so every join’s keys sit in the leftmost table of both sides. A check confirms that the new plan keeps every table, joins on the same keys and has no cross product."],
    ["A cost model from table sizes", "For the Stack Overflow and IMDB benchmarks it stores each table’s base size and the entity each join column refers to. From these it estimates how much each semijoin shrinks its parent, and prices a plan as tuples hashed, at three times the cost, plus tuples probed."],
    ["Searching join trees", "A hill climb swaps tree edges for others on the same keys, tries every table as the root and orders each node’s children by how selective they are, most selective first. It evaluates at most 6,000 candidate trees."],
    ["A rule for unknown schemas", "On any other schema, it roots the tree at the largest input so that this input is never hashed, and hangs children with the same keys under the first of them, so their parent is probed once. Any failure falls back to a plain conversion."],
  ]},
};
